      {
        "children": [
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": ""
              }
            ],
            "id": "3449587084",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "alt": "A photograph shows an aerial view of buildings, trees and a large area clear of vegetation, \n            above which rises a plume of steam.",
            "caption": [
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "Geysers are a dramatic display of thermodynamic principles in nature. Water deep within the underground channels of the geyser is under high pressure and heated to high temperature by magma. When a pocket of water near the surface reaches boiling point and is expelled, the resulting drop in pressure causes larger volumes of water to flash boil, forcefully ejecting steam and water in an impressive eruption. (Credit: Modification of work by Yellowstone National Park.)"
                  }
                ],
                "type": "p"
              }
            ],
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "x8or04nim7rp6cl",
                "text": " ",
                "type": "text"
              }
            ],
            "id": "etabhaeyoueudzo",
            "src": "https://d2xvti2irp4c7t.cloudfront.net/media/0e/0ed52161a66a025462890369d0dd0826/therm_intro_pic.PNG",
            "type": "img"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "Hot coffee cools down, iron objects rust, plants undergo photosynthesis, and adenosine triphosphate (ATP) molecules provide the energy for many biochemical reactions in our cells. Why do these processes—and in general, why do any processes—occur? "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "Thermodynamics"
                  }
                ],
                "content": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": "The branch of science that deals with the relationships between heat, work, temperature, energy, and entropy."
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "2081967314",
                    "type": "p"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "1947557393",
                "trigger": "hover",
                "type": "popup"
              },
              {
                "text": ", the study of the relationships between the energy and work associated with chemical and physical processes, enables us to predict whether a process will occur under specified conditions. "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "Thermochemistry"
                  }
                ],
                "content": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": "A branch of thermodynamics that studies the heat released or absorbed during chemical reactions. "
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "3684727296",
                    "type": "p"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "3620451465",
                "trigger": "hover",
                "type": "popup"
              },
              {
                "text": " is an important aspect of thermodynamics concerned with the quantity of heat accompanying chemical reactions and phase transitions. "
              }
            ],
            "id": "aj6zubw27xztnfs",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "We have already learned about the "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "first law of thermodynamics"
                  }
                ],
                "content": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": "A formulation of the law of conservation of energy; the internal energy of a system changes due to heat flow in or out of the system and work done on or by the system."
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "1999255889",
                    "type": "p"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "2744493666",
                "trigger": "hover",
                "type": "popup"
              },
              {
                "text": ", which describes the conservation of energy, and how to use internal energy ("
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "E"
              },
              {
                "text": ") and enthalpy ("
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "H"
              },
              {
                "text": ") to keep track of energy changes. This unit discusses additional thermodynamic concepts related to the direction of chemical and physical change. We will encounter entropy ("
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "S"
              },
              {
                "text": "), a quantity related to the randomness or \"disorder\" of a system. This leads us to the second law of thermodynamics, which enables us to predict whether a chemical or physical change has a tendency to occur—is \"spontaneous\"—under a given set of conditions. This law is of central importance in chemistry and provides the foundation for equilibrium, electrochemistry, and other subdisciplines. We will also introduce Gibbs free energy ("
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "G"
              },
              {
                "text": "), which is related to the enthalpy, entropy, and temperature of a system. This thermodynamic quantity allows us to evaluate the spontaneity of a process solely in terms of the process and its conditions."
              }
            ],
            "id": "99jrcxfhwpcb5ke",
            "type": "p"
          }
        ],
